# ---- speaker notes of the frame slides
NOTES = {
    "title": "Welcome. This chapter is the prompt, the values it computes with, the operators that combine them, the names that remember them, and a first program. Every concept gets an idea, a reason and a picture first, then the code, then what the run printed.",
    "route": "Four parts: values and types, operations, names and functions, and a first program. Each range is a run of slides you can jump to. The chapter-code tab of the page holds the same code in the same order. Ask the class which part they expect to find hardest, and why.",
    "questions": "Three questions to carry through the chapter. Which operations can raise an error, and which error? Which results can surprise a student? And what has changed in current Python since the book? Ask students to write a guess for each now and check it at the end.",
    "divider1": "Part one. The shell evaluates an expression and prints its value, and every value has a data type: integer, float or string. Example: seven plus one point zero is a float. Ask the class which result in this part they expect to surprise them.",
    "divider2": "Part two. Operators combine values, precedence decides who goes first, and parentheses override it. Plus and star also work on strings, with a different meaning. Example: two plus three times six. Ask the class to predict it before the run.",
    "divider3": "Part three. A variable is a name for a value. Print shows, input reads, and str, int, float and len convert and measure. Errors are answers too. Example: adding text to a number. Ask the class what Python will say before you run it.",
    "divider4": "Part four. A first program uses everything so far. Then we take it apart line by line, and finish with the binary digits behind every number. Example: the greeting program. Ask the class which earlier idea they see in each of its lines.",
    "A1": "A calculator with a screen is the shell. An expression is a recipe line that ends in one value. A data type is the label on a container. Example: the crate, the jug and the sign. Ask students to add an everyday picture of their own for a data type.",
    "A2": "Operators are verbs between values. Precedence is the order of work in a kitchen: chop before you stir. Go through the eight pictures one by one. Example: folding paper doubles it, which is the power. Ask which picture the students remember best.",
    "A3": "Concatenation glues strips of text, and replication photocopies them. The function str is the label maker that turns any value into text. Example: gluing Alice to Bob. Ask the class why a glue stick cannot join a strip of paper to a number.",
    "A4": "A variable is a labelled jar: the label is the name, the contents are the value. Print is the loudspeaker, and input is the suggestion box. Example: a number dropped in the box arrives as paper. Ask the class why input gives text.",
    "A5": "Len is a ruler for text. Int and float are machines with a slot for the input and a slot for the result. Import fetches a toolbox from the shelf. Example: the machine int reads the text forty-two. Ask which machine rejects the text four point two.",
    "A6": "A program is a recipe: steps in order, ingredients in labelled jars. Dissecting it is taking a clock apart, and binary is a row of light switches. Example: five is the switches for four and one. Ask the class which picture helps them most.",
    "current": "Python moves on. The shell and its error messages improved in 3.13, and the free-threaded build is experimental. F-strings have a formal grammar since 3.12. Example: PEP 701. Ask the class to check one row against its source before trusting it.",
    "summary": "Five sentences to keep, and the chart counts the code examples of each part. Everything is on the chapter page, in the same order. Example: the first sentence, the shell prints one value. Ask each student to say which sentence they still doubt.",
    "practice": "Four drills. Predict first, then run on the page. Example: seven floor-divided by two, times two, plus seven remainder two. Ask students to explain each answer in one sentence to a neighbour, and to name the rule that decided it.",
    "resources": "Start with the book chapter and the Python tutorial, then the video by Guido van Rossum. The stories link to first-hand sources. Example: the floating-point page explains zero point one plus zero point two. Ask each student to choose one link to read before the next class.",
}

